{"rule":"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged. The text field holds, in at most 1800 characters, everything a reader would need to know of the story so far, plus one new event that moves the story forward. It is written for a human reader.","text":"At Vardnes, the tide was arriving increasingly early. Marit Solvang, forty-seven, noticed the latest discrepancy at 13:09, fifty-one minutes before the predicted high tide, when her mussel bucket disappeared beneath the water. Anders Holm, the lighthouse keeper who had left on March 2, had documented the drift in his logbook since the previous July: first four minutes, then nine, then twenty-two. His cottage key still hung behind Marit's door. The cottage barometer had held at 1003 for a week. Now the sea had reached her shed doorstep, forcing her to haul the outboard motor into the loft, and a dead gull lay on the shingle.

Marit's grandmother, Signe, had kept the tide table for forty years. With Anders gone, Marit decided to take his logbook to the cottage before dark and look for something that might explain the discrepancy. The ferry Kittiwake would pass the point at 16:40 on Tuesday and Friday; otherwise, her next regular contact beyond the village was her brother Tomas's Sunday call from Bergen.

At the cottage, she found a folded sheet tucked into the drawer where Anders kept his charts. It was a tide table in Signe's handwriting, dated thirty-eight years earlier. Beside a high tide crossed out and corrected to fifty-one minutes early, her grandmother had written: 'Next comes the withdrawal. Do not follow the water.'"}
